<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  What macOS has to be told before it will let this app ask.

  A usage string is not a nicety: without one the system refuses the request
  outright rather than showing anybody a prompt, so the feature fails in a way
  that looks like a bug in the app. Dictation needs two -- the microphone to
  hear, and speech recognition to turn what it heard into words -- and reading
  calendars needs its own.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Errand uses the microphone when you dictate an errand instead of typing it.</string>
  <key>NSSpeechRecognitionUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Errand turns what you dictate into the words of an errand.</string>
  <!--
    Calendars, read through EventKit, which sees every repeat of a repeating
    event; the Calendar app's scripting sees only the first. Full access is
    the newer name for reading them, and the older key is what macOS 12 and
    13 read instead.
  -->
  <key>NSCalendarsFullAccessUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Errand reads your calendars when an agent is asked what is on, or is set to wake before an event. It never adds, moves or cancels anything.</string>
  <key>NSCalendarsUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Errand reads your calendars when an agent is asked what is on, or is set to wake before an event. It never adds, moves or cancels anything.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
